 ## Overview
-3D Half-Gaussian Splatting (3D-HGS) modifies the standard 3D Gaussian kernel into a half-ellipsoid to better model sharp surface boundaries and occlusions, addressing the "blooming" artifacts common in full Gaussians near object edges [S1]. It achieves this by transforming the covariance matrix into a local basis aligned with a splitting plane normal, projecting the covariance to enforce zero variance along the normal direction, and then transforming it back to world space [S1]. This method builds on [Regularization and Geometry Constraints](6269-regularization-and-geometry-constraints.md) by applying a structural constraint to the Gaussian parameters themselves rather than an auxiliary loss term.
+3D Half-Gaussian Splatting (3D-HGS) modifies the standard 3D Gaussian kernel into a half-ellipsoid to better model sharp surface boundaries and occlusions, effectively representing the sharp transitions between object surfaces and empty space [S1]. It achieves this by transforming the covariance matrix into a local basis aligned with a splitting plane normal, projecting the covariance to enforce near-zero variance along the normal direction, and then transforming it back to world space [S1]. This method builds on [Regularization and Geometry Constraints](6269-regularization-and-geometry-constraints.md) by applying a structural constraint to the Gaussian parameters themselves rather than an auxiliary loss term [G].
 
 ## How it works
-The method replaces the standard 3D Gaussian ellipsoid with a half-Gaussian defined by a splitting plane. The core operation is a covariance transformation that projects the 3D covariance onto the tangent plane of the splitting normal, effectively creating a flat, half-ellipsoidal shape.
-
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Input**: The original 3D Gaussian covariance matrix $\Sigma$ (3x3 symmetric positive definite), and the splitting plane normal vector $n$ (3x1).
-*   **Output**: The regularized covariance matrix $\sigma$ (3x3), representing the half-Gaussian.
+*   **Input**: The original 3D Gaussian covariance matrix $\Sigma$ (3x3 symmetric positive definite) and the splitting plane normal vector $n$ (3x1) [S1].
+*   **Output**: The regularized covariance matrix $\sigma$ (3x3) representing the half-Gaussian [S1].
 
